part.think:
Important encoder output:

```
return {
\t["a"] = 1;
\t["b"] = "x";
\t["c"] = {
\t\t1,
\t\t2,
\t};
};
```

So default encoder:
- `return ` prefix, `;\n` suffix.
- Map: `{\n\t["key"] = value;\n...}` — keys as `["a"]` quoted-bracket form by default (LuaUnquoted false).
- Map entries separated by `;` (with newline after each).
- Seq: `{\n\t\t1,\n\t\t2,\n}` — items separated by `,`, trailing comma after last item! `2,` then `\n\t};`. Note map entries: last entry `["c"] = {...};` also has trailing `;`.
- Indent is TAB, not spaces! `^I` = tab. And s.Indent probably controls... probe -I4.
- Empty map from `{}`: probe.

Seq detection: {[1],[2],[4]} → map with keys 1,2,4 (gap → map). {[0],[1]} → map. {[1],[2],[3]} any order → seq. So seq iff keys are exactly {1..n}.

Wait {[1.5],[1]} → map with keys "1" then "1.5" — sorted numerically 1 < 1.5. Good.

Now probe more encoder: nested maps, scalars at root, strings escaping, --lua-unquoted, --lua-globals, prefix/suffix, indent, empty map/seq, null, multiple docs.

